WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.820
Who in our company can make customer
data public, and would anyone notice?

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.470
Four settings cause most cloud breaches.

00:00:08.917 --> 00:00:12.087
First, a storage bucket
open to the whole internet.

00:00:12.397 --> 00:00:17.367
Someone needs to share a file, a deadline
is close, and they make the bucket public.

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:19.592
The setting is never changed back.

00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:24.327
Scanners sweep the internet for exactly
this, and can find it within hours.

00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:26.177
Nobody attacked us.

00:00:26.352 --> 00:00:27.602
We published it ourselves.

00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:31.387
Second, an access key in the source code.

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:36.607
A static key grants access without a
person, a password or a second factor.

00:00:36.852 --> 00:00:40.922
If the repository is public for
a few minutes, bots copy the key.

00:00:41.222 --> 00:00:43.512
They use it before anyone removes it.

00:00:43.747 --> 00:00:46.897
The fastest outcome is a
bill for hundreds of servers.

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:51.013
Third, one role that can
do everything, everywhere.

00:00:51.358 --> 00:00:54.588
A cloud estate can be split
into many separate accounts.

00:00:54.943 --> 00:00:57.053
That separation is cheap security,

00:00:57.443 --> 00:00:58.753
if nothing bridges it.

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:03.103
Usually one role does: an administrator,
or a deployment pipeline.

00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:08.273
Whoever holds that credential holds
the entire company, backups included.

00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:11.933
Fourth, a region where nobody is watching.

00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:13.373
With a stolen key,

00:01:13.743 --> 00:01:16.133
the intruder picks the
region we never open.

00:01:16.423 --> 00:01:20.133
Logging there was never switched on,
so nothing raises an alert.

00:01:20.468 --> 00:01:23.858
The finance team finds them first,
when the invoice doubles.

00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:26.533
And we cannot tell a
regulator that we checked.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:28.860
What good looks like.

00:01:29.145 --> 00:01:34.005
A landing zone creates every new account
with logging and guardrails already in

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:34.535
place.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:39.945
A policy at organisation level makes
public storage impossible to save.

00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:43.365
Workloads use identities
instead of static keys.

00:01:43.755 --> 00:01:46.705
Each workload gets a role
for its own job only.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.360
All accounts and regions
log to one central place.

00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:52.435
These are settings, not products.

00:01:52.895 --> 00:01:55.765
NIS2 lists access control
among its measures,

00:01:56.195 --> 00:01:58.065
and the configuration stays ours.

00:01:59.997 --> 00:02:01.207
A suggested first step.

00:02:01.492 --> 00:02:05.082
Ask who can change a setting
that makes customer data public.

00:02:05.437 --> 00:02:07.487
And would anyone notice the same day?

00:02:07.732 --> 00:02:12.562
If the answers are several dozen people,
and probably not, that is the risk,

00:02:12.572 --> 00:02:13.282
not the cloud.

00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:16.507
Four settings, one rule.

00:02:16.807 --> 00:02:18.557
The provider secures the building.

00:02:18.882 --> 00:02:20.112
We lock our own doors.

